(5294458, 768484, 30364, '<td valign="top">That is VERY good.  It''ll frighten the crap out of the coulrophobics, as your title suggests.  Instead of the usual two-dimensional ''evil clown'' character, this one has an unnerving depth to him ... cool and disdainful.  Thus we have a clown who is clearly <i>not</i> a clown; unsettling indeed.  And it''s an impression heightened by the schizophrenic character of your lighting. <br/>I give it a 10.  And the wildly capricious Order of the Thumb.<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=748778" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg"/></a></td>', 'That is VERY good.  It''ll frighten the crap out of the coulrophobics, as your title suggests.  Instead of the usual two-dimensional ''evil clown'' character, this one has an unnerving depth to him ... cool and disdainful.  Thus we have a clown who is clearly not a clown; unsettling indeed.  And it''s an impression heightened by the schizophrenic character of your lighting. I give it a 10.  And the wildly capricious Order of the Thumb.', '2009-03-07 16:44:09.000000', NULL, 1),
(5294462, 768747, 42100, '<td valign="top">Nice clarity with the bird, and the beautiful bokeh compliments very nicely!</td>', 'Nice clarity with the bird, and the beautiful bokeh compliments very nicely!', '2009-03-07 16:47:55.000000', NULL, 1),
(5294465, 768762, 30364, '<td valign="top">Sometimes the simplest juxtapositions are the hardest to see.  This huge abstract superstructure towering above the tiny human at the desk makes a startling example.  It''s impossible to just note the presence of the man and say "Oh!'', and accept this image at face value ... it''s too laden with figurative possibilities.  If I were a writer (which I am) it''s the sort of image that could inspire me to write a novel, just so this could be on the cover.  I might call it <i>Have a Nice Day</i>.  Or perhaps <i>Your Call is Important to Us.</i>  Of course, there''s nothing much wrong with <i>Information</i>.  <br/>I award it a 10.  And the deeply dubious distinction of The Order of the Thumb.<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=748778" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg"/></a></td>', 'Sometimes the simplest juxtapositions are the hardest to see.  This huge abstract superstructure towering above the tiny human at the desk makes a startling example.  It''s impossible to just note the presence of the man and say "Oh!'', and accept this image at face value ... it''s too laden with figurative possibilities.  If I were a writer (which I am) it''s the sort of image that could inspire me to write a novel, just so this could be on the cover.  I might call it Have a Nice Day.  Or perhaps Your Call is Important to Us.  Of course, there''s nothing much wrong with Information.  I award it a 10.  And the deeply dubious distinction of The Order of the Thumb.', '2009-03-07 16:48:58.000000', NULL, 1),
(5294466, 768771, 30364, '<td valign="top">A very lovely photograph made all the more engaging by the ''imperfections'' (the imposition of the foreground shape, the movement blur, the various ''distractions'', even the slightly cool color temp) ... these all emphasize the fact that this wonderful moment was not made for the photographer''s benefit, but for joy''s.  Photographs that are ''perfect'' are very often diminished by that flawlessness, and thus speak less honestly.  Truly great photographs are never about creating moments; they''re about celebrating moments.  Just like this one.  <br/>10.  And the Order of the Thumb.<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=748778" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/30000-34999/30364/120/748778.jpg"/></a></td>', 'A very lovely photograph made all the more engaging by the ''imperfections'' (the imposition of the foreground shape, the movement blur, the various ''distractions'', even the slightly cool color temp) ... these all emphasize the fact that this wonderful moment was not made for the photographer''s benefit, but for joy''s.  Photographs that are ''perfect'' are very often diminished by that flawlessness, and thus speak less honestly.  Truly great photographs are never about creating moments; they''re about celebrating moments.  Just like this one.  10.  And the Order of the Thumb.', '2009-03-07 16:49:30.000000', NULL, 1),
